## Dynamic Arrays with Vec

`Vec[T]` (vector) is a growable array — a list that can expand and shrink at runtime. It's the most commonly used collection in XIOM.

### Creating and Using Vec

```xiom
let numbers = Vec[Int]();    // empty vector
numbers.push(10);            // add to end
numbers.push(20);
numbers.push(30);
// numbers: [10, 20, 30]
```

### Anatomy of Vec Operations

```xiom
let v = Vec[Int]();
v.push(1);          // add element
v.push(2);
v.push(3);
let last = v.pop();  // remove and return last element (Some(3))
let next = v.pop();  // Some(2)
let empty = v.pop(); // None — only one element left
```

`.pop()` returns `Option[T]` — `Some(value)` when there's an element, `None` when empty.

### Creating with Initial Values

```xiom
// push each individually
let v = Vec[Int]();
v.push(1); v.push(2); v.push(3);

// Or use a helper function
fn make_vec() -> Vec[Int] {
  let v = Vec[Int]();
  v.push(1); v.push(2); v.push(3);
  return v;
}
```

### Common Patterns

```xiom
// Build a Vec in a loop
let squares = Vec[Int]();
var i = 1;
while i <= 5 {
  squares.push(i * i);
  i = i + 1;
}
// squares: [1, 4, 9, 16, 25]

// Collect filtered items
let evens = Vec[Int]();
var j = 0;
while j <= 10 {
  if j % 2 == 0 {
    evens.push(j);
  }
  j = j + 1;
}
```

### Vec on the Heap

Vec stores its elements on the heap, so assignment **moves** ownership:

```xiom
let a = Vec[Int]();
a.push(1);
let b = a;         // a moved to b — a is invalid now
b.push(2);         // OK
// a.push(3);      // ERROR: a was moved
```

